WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:11.581
On 8 August 2008, the same day that the 2008
Beijing Olympic Games kicked off, the Russian

00:00:11.581 --> 00:00:16.519
army launched its full-scale invasion of Georgia.

00:00:16.519 --> 00:00:22.560
The most important event of the decade for
China, the Olympic Games that were to crown

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.460
the process of the full incorporation of the
communist country into the international community,

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:31.259
was to be marred by Vladimir Putin and his
warmongering aspirations.

00:00:31.259 --> 00:00:34.840
At that time, Xi Jinping was not yet at the
helm of China.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.610
Instead it was his predecessor, Hu Jintao,
who reacted discreetly, but was deeply irritated

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:43.559
with Moscow, because of the date chosen for
the invasion: the same day that the Olympic

00:00:43.559 --> 00:00:50.149
flame would light up China for the first time.

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:54.590
It seems, VisualPolitik community, that although
much has happened since then, some habits

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:55.810
never change.

00:00:55.810 --> 00:01:00.449
As if it were a kind of déjà vu, Putin made
the decision to invade Ukraine just at the

00:01:00.449 --> 00:01:05.680
time when China was hosting another Olympic
Games, the Beijing 2022 Winter Games.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.520
In fact, it is believed that Xi Jinping asked
the Russian leader to postpone his invasion

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.350
until after the closing of the games.

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:16.150
And whether or not this is true, the point
is that only four days after the closing of

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:18.860
these games, Putin's hordes were launched
on Ukraine.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:22.960
Since then, the attitude of the People's Republic
of China has been tremendously ambiguous and

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:23.960
erratic.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.540
But one thing has become very clear:

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:33.790
Xi Jinping is moving further and further away
from his predecessors.

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:37.540
During all this time, throughout a year of
war and despite the terrible crimes committed

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:42.219
by the Russian army, the Chinese dictator
has remained silent, on the sidelines...

00:01:42.219 --> 00:01:43.280
At least, until now.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.030
This image will surely take a long time to
be forgotten.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:54.520
Yes, the image that some doubted would happen
and that others announced as inevitable finally

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.520
came to pass.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.040
The dictators of China and Russia, Xi Jinping
and Vladimir Putin, together at a bilateral

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.880
summit in Moscow, just one year after the
start of the war.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.060
It was not the first time they have met since
the beginning of the invasion, but it was

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.530
the first time they met at the highest level
and within the framework of a bilateral summit.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:14.750
Perhaps for this reason, many have interpreted
Xi's visit to Moscow as an endorsement of

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:19.180
Putin, a way to legitimize the Russian leader
on the international scene and, perhaps, also

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.180
to whitewash his operations.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:25.340
Even more so when the visit took place only
a few days after news stories like this one

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:26.379
broke.

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:31.510
(ICC judges issue arrest warrant for Vladimir
Putin over alleged war crimes – The Guardian)

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:32.820
However, this is only the footnote.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:36.870
The truth is that Xi Jinping's visit to Russia
is about more than just handshakes or forced

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:37.870
smiles.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:42.180
It was, above all, Beijing's latest attempt
to become the capital most antagonistic to

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:43.790
Washington once and for all.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:46.319
The clear alternative to Western leadership.

00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:49.420
Now, does all this mean that China will ultimately
side with Russia?

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:52.250
Is Beijing really as neutral as they claim
to be?

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:56.640
What on earth are the true Chinese intentions
with this apparent rapprochement with Putin?

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.060
Who has the upper hand in this whole story?

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:05.540
VisualPolitik viewers, in this video we are
going to answer these questions so keep watching

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:08.640
this video.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.290
(THE 'MULTIPOLAR ORDER')

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:17.390
Take a look at this striking moment.

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:21.980
Here you see Wang Yi, head of the Chinese
Communist Party's Central Commission for Foreign

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:26.810
Affairs, Xi Jinping's top foreign policy advisor,
together with representatives of Saudi Arabia

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:27.810
and Iran.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:32.690
Two countries that in theory hate each other
have now decided to re-establish diplomatic

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:33.690
relations.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.190
And they have done so thanks to China's mediation.

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:39.840
It is not very common to see the Chinese as
mediators in international problems, but it

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.319
seems that now this is precisely the new path
that the Chinese regime wants to follow.

00:03:44.319 --> 00:03:49.879
And the conflict between Russia and Ukraine
seems to be his next target.

00:03:49.879 --> 00:03:56.030
At the end of February 2023, coinciding with
the first anniversary of the war, China presented

00:03:56.030 --> 00:04:00.850
a twelve-point plan to, in theory, provide
a political solution to the conflict.

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:03.640
In reality this was something of a take-it-or-leave-it
plan.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:07.439
They didn't even consult Ukraine's opinion
on the twelve points presented.

00:04:07.439 --> 00:04:08.550
Nor are they very concise.

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:12.879
In fact, most of the points include extremely
general things like respecting the sovereignty

00:04:12.879 --> 00:04:16.759
of all countries, ceasing hostilities and
reducing strategic risks.

00:04:16.759 --> 00:04:24.360
The important thing is that with this document
China already had its peace plan.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:28.120
Theoretically now the Chinese are going to
start working to see how these points can

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:29.170
be realized.

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:34.580
And that is surely what explains news items
like this in the Chinese media:

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:42.199
("European leaders' trips to China highlight
Beijing's role as a 'peace promoter'.

00:04:42.199 --> 00:05:02.110
– Global Times)But, of course, the doubt,
the big question mark in this whole story

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:03.110
is...

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:06.340
Can we really consider China as a neutral
player between Russia and Ukraine capable

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:11.090
of acting as a mediating country in this conflict?

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:14.240
Well, what can I say?

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:32.949
Words like these don't help:
And I don't know about you, but, for Xi to

00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:37.390
tell Putin that they are driving changes together,
doesn't seem like the best cover letter for

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:38.810
becoming a mediator.

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:42.819
However, just a moment, because to stop at
these images would be a terribly simple and

00:05:42.819 --> 00:05:45.570
unfortunate analysis of this whole situation.

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:47.449
Beijing talks about its peace plan...

00:05:47.449 --> 00:05:50.880
But what if China really doesn't care if the
war ends soon?

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:52.130
Yes, yes, you heard correctly.

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:53.740
That is the question.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:56.960
Let me explain.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:00.539
Initially, this war was a nuisance for China:

00:06:00.539 --> 00:06:05.130
Suddenly everyone began to worry about national
security.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:08.690
Western support for Ukraine overcame barriers
that could be considered a precedent for other

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:10.259
situations, like Taiwan, for instance.

00:06:10.259 --> 00:06:14.140
The United States greatly improved its international
standing and had the luxury of restricting

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:16.380
the sale of advanced technology to China itself.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:20.810
And to top it all off, the global economic
slowdown contributed to China's economic growth

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:24.490
in 2022 falling to almost half of the government's
target.

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:27.160
However, where there's a will, there's a way.

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:31.190
Take note because right now, we will identify
at least three main reasons why things may

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:35.069
not be seen by the Chinese regime the same
way as we think.

00:06:35.069 --> 00:06:37.380
The first of these has to do with dough.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:41.640
The extraordinary economic benefits China
is getting from Western sanctions on Russia.

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:44.210
Do you want a concrete example of all this?

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:51.500
Then take a look at this graph:
Of course, energy is the key driver of China's

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:54.630
imports.

00:06:54.630 --> 00:07:00.020
The war, sanctions and European disengagement
from Russia have allowed China to buy record

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:04.389
amounts of gas and oil at deep discounts,
just when its economy is not at its best.

00:07:04.389 --> 00:07:08.040
Russia needs to sell as much as it can, even
if that means selling much more cheaply.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:14.069
And so, in the months of January and February
2023, with an increase of almost 24% year-on-year,

00:07:14.069 --> 00:07:18.990
Russia unseated Saudi Arabia as China's largest
oil supplier.

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:26.009
Of course, Russia cannot flood China with
oil and gas, basically because a) it lacks

00:07:26.009 --> 00:07:30.840
the infrastructure and b) unlike the Germans,
the Chinese have always been very wary of

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:31.840
their energy security.

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:36.639
At the beginning of 2023, Russia's share of
Chinese purchases was already above 20%.

00:07:36.639 --> 00:07:38.850
So the margin seems small.

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:43.810
However, the idea now is to replace purchases
using ships with gas sent via pipelines.

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:47.349
We are talking, for instance, about trying
to speed up the deadlines for the construction

00:07:47.349 --> 00:07:52.360
of Power Of Siberia 2, a new gas pipeline
that could transport, through more than 2,600

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:57.340
km, up to 50 billion cubic meters of gas,
which would double the current Russian gas

00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:00.250
export capacity to China.

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:07.290
Of course, there is one problem: Chinese estimates
are to import 38 billion cubic meters of gas

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:12.289
by 2025, while the Russians aspire to be able
to sell them 98 bcm by 2030.

00:08:12.289 --> 00:08:14.639
And with oil it is a very similar story.

00:08:14.639 --> 00:08:19.099
Let's just say there is a massive divide between
what China wants to buy and what Russia wants

00:08:19.099 --> 00:08:20.099
to sell.

00:08:20.099 --> 00:08:23.310
So, for the Chinese to agree to further increase
their energy exposure to Russia, the Kremlin

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:27.139
will have to commit to much lower prices,
give guarantees and jump through the hoops

00:08:27.139 --> 00:08:29.060
of whatever King Xi says.

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:33.270
Because don't forget one thing: this is not
a relationship between equals.

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:37.779
The "special relationship" between Russia
and China may end up making the former a kind

00:08:37.779 --> 00:08:39.729
of vassal State of the latter.

00:08:39.729 --> 00:08:43.760
And this is in fact the second great advantage
that the war could have for China: a much

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:45.240
greater political influence.

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:51.660
A new degree of influence that they themselves
have dubbed: the multipolar order.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:57.920
The idea of the multipolar order is to bring
together the power spheres of Russia and China,

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:02.450
the Russophile and Sinophile world, to create
a new global power axis to serve as a counterweight

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:04.070
to the US in all fields.

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:08.240
The small detail is that now, with the enormous
weakening that Russia is suffering, China

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:12.709
could take advantage of that situation to
impose its geopolitical, economic and military

00:09:12.709 --> 00:09:18.269
interests over any Russian demands.

00:09:18.269 --> 00:09:19.910
Any examples of where this could go?

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:23.630
Well, one of the possible scenarios in which
China could use Russia to consolidate its

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:25.200
power would be in Africa.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:29.050
But it is not only about the geostrategic
field when we talk about Chinese influence.

00:09:29.050 --> 00:09:31.510
There is also the economic field.

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:32.510
Take a look.

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:37.580
(Putin told Xi Russia's ready to switch to
yuans in foreign trade – Yahoo News)

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:40.820
Basically, what this change would imply is
that Russia would stop trading in dollars

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:44.800
with countries in Asia, Latin America and
Africa, switching to the Renminbi, which is

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:51.030
the name of China's currency commonly known
as the yuan.

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:55.839
In fact, in 2022, the yuan's share of Russian
export payments increased from 0.5 percent

00:09:55.839 --> 00:09:56.839
to 16 percent.

00:09:56.839 --> 00:10:01.029
And, for example, in February 2023, the yuan
overtook the dollar for the first time in

00:10:01.029 --> 00:10:05.050
history as the most traded currency on the
Moscow Stock Exchange, with almost 40 percent

00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:06.050
of transactions.

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:08.990
It is what many have already called the Petroyuan...

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:11.300
Of course, its consequences have been greatly
exaggerated.

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:13.910
The yuan is not capable of replacing the dollar.

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:14.910
Why?

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:18.160
Basically because it lacks legal certainty
and because China maintains strict capital

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:19.160
controls.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:22.500
But even so, this change could boost China's
influence in Russia and in general throughout

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:24.590
the entire Central Asian region.

00:10:24.590 --> 00:10:30.120
Finally, there is the military issue.

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:34.480
Russia is suffering an enormous military attrition,
which along with the sanctions is having two

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:35.899
very clear consequences:

00:10:35.899 --> 00:10:39.760
On the one hand, Russia's military capability
has been sapped, virtually leaving China as

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:42.050
the sole major military power in Central Asia.

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:45.510
On the other hand, when the war is over and
Russia needs to acquire new military equipment

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:49.050
to replace its losses, if the sanctions are
not lifted, they are likely to turn to the

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:51.520
Chinese military industry for hardware and
components.

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:52.920
And it’s not just Russia.

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:57.040
A good many of Russia's customers could gradually
shift to Chinese industry.

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:02.589
So there you have it, these would be the three
ways in which China could aspire to benefit

00:11:02.589 --> 00:11:03.680
from all this mess.

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:07.580
Three core ideas with which Xi Jinping seems
to want to make up for the great blow that

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:08.780
the war has caused them.

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:13.610
And it is right here that we come to a big
dilemma.

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:17.130
Listen up.

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:20.320
(THE GREAT DILEMMA)

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:27.170
After what we have told you in this video,
if you had to answer the question we asked

00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:31.430
you at the beginning about whether or not
China is really neutral in regards to Russia,

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:33.089
what answer would you give?

00:11:33.089 --> 00:11:36.910
China does not have a neutral position, basically
because Xi Jinping has found a path in the

00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:40.490
war to push his particular agenda of confrontation
with the West.

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:44.550
Also, don't forget that the Chinese economy
is starting to experience a lot of problems,

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:47.350
so there’s nothing better than having a
scapegoat on hand.

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:51.610
You know how it goes, no, we're not to blame
for the problems, it's because of the war.

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:53.710
However, this is not the whole story.

00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:56.820
Right here we have arrived at the great dilemma
facing the Chinese government.

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:00.990
On the one hand, as we have seen, the war
may have benefits for Xi Jinping's new agenda,

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:06.490
but, on the other hand, it also involves many
problems that media outlets usually overlook:

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:13.130
For example, the war is causing its Asia-Pacific
neighbors to increase their military spending

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:15.649
and move even closer to the United States.

00:12:15.649 --> 00:12:19.800
This may structurally weaken China's ambitions
to become the dominant power in the Asia-Pacific.

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:24.540
At the same time, it is also contributing
to the further deterioration of China's already

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:27.910
strained economic, trade and political relations
with the West.

00:12:27.910 --> 00:12:31.260
Which is exactly what China does not need
at the moment.

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:34.829
Let's just say that the war has made many
governments perceive dictatorships like Putin's

00:12:34.829 --> 00:13:10.450
and Xi Jinping's as untrustworthy.

00:13:10.450 --> 00:13:38.110
To make matters worse, the war is also slowing
global economic growth, a growth that China

00:13:38.110 --> 00:13:41.910
needs more than ever if it is to overcome
all the problems it faces: we are talking

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:45.720
about things like the bursting of the real
estate bubble, the technology crisis, banking

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:49.449
problems, hyper-indebtedness of local governments,
and so on and so forth.

00:13:49.449 --> 00:13:53.430
And, of course, if they fail to overcome all
these imbalances, the new multipolar world

00:13:53.430 --> 00:14:00.470
order of which they dream so much could be
a very short-lived one.

00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:04.639
It seems that Xi Jinping has dramatically
turned the rudder with respect to his predecessors...

00:14:04.639 --> 00:14:07.670
And the results have not been particularly
good.

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:12.029
Now this seems to be their latest gamble,
to try to cash in on the war Russia has provoked

00:14:12.029 --> 00:14:13.029
in Ukraine.

00:14:13.029 --> 00:14:16.920
Of course, it is not clear that China can
really derive any benefit from this game.

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:21.720
What is clear is that it is neither a neutral
country nor a reliable regime.

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:24.690
But for now the questions are over to you:

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:27.720
Do you think China will manage to benefit
from the current situation?

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:31.509
Will Russia end up becoming a kind of vassal
State of Beijing?

00:14:31.509 --> 00:14:34.529
What future do you predict for the alliance
between China and Russia?

00:14:34.529 --> 00:14:37.660
Will they really be able to promote a new
world order?

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:41.089
Leave us your thoughts below and if you found
this video interesting don't forget to like

00:14:41.089 --> 00:14:46.269
it and subscribe to VisualPolitik and Patreon
to keep up to date with all our content.

00:14:46.269 --> 00:14:47.269
Thank you very much for watching.

00:14:47.269 --> 00:14:48.510
All the best and see you next time.

